<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Urban IV</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1195–<a href="/years/1264">1264</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Jacques Pantaléon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Urban IV, born Jacques Pantaléon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, was pope from 1261 to 1264, a former archdeacon of Liège and patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He spent much of his reign at Orvieto and Viterbo, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> to take the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, and in 1264 extended the feast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> to the whole Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv.jpg" alt="Pope Urban IV" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taddeo_di_bartolo,_s._tommaso_d%27aquino_presenta_la_sua_liturgia_del_corpus_christi_a_urbano_IV,_1403_ca._(cropped2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
